## sc-disc | Accidental discoveries | physics | 0 | - | ev-phys
story 4 | Röntgen discovered X-rays by accident, 1895
- Experimenting with cathode rays in a darkened lab, he noticed a fluorescent screen glowing across the room.
- Rays passed through card, wood, and flesh, but not bone.
- *The first X-ray image was his wife's hand, wedding ring and all. She reportedly said, "I have seen my death."*
> Radioactivity was discovered on a cloudy day

story 4 | Becquerel's cloudy-day discovery
- 1896: Becquerel thought uranium salts needed sunlight to emit rays, but cloudy weather stopped his experiment.
- He developed the photographic plates anyway. They were strongly fogged.
- *Uranium emitted radiation on its own: radioactivity.*
> Teflon was a failed refrigerant

story 3 | Teflon was found in a "broken" gas cylinder
- 1938: Roy Plunkett at DuPont found a cylinder of refrigerant gas that seemed empty but weighed full.
- Cutting it open revealed a slippery white solid: PTFE.
- *Later used in non-stick pans and the Manhattan Project.*
> The microwave oven, superglue and Post-it notes

story 3 | Post-it notes came from a failed glue
- 1968: Spencer Silver at 3M made a weak adhesive that stuck lightly and peeled off cleanly. No one wanted it.
- His colleague Art Fry used it to stop bookmarks falling out of his hymn book.
- *Post-its launched nationally in 1980.*
> Next: scientists' lives

## sc-lives | Scientists' lives | history | 0 | - | hi-phys
story 4 | Tycho Brahe had a metal nose
- He lost part of his nose in a 1566 duel, reportedly over a mathematical dispute, and wore a brass or gold-silver prosthetic.
- He also kept a pet elk, which reportedly died after drinking too much beer and falling down stairs.
- *His precise observations made Kepler's laws possible.*
> Faraday left school at 14

story 4 | Faraday was a bookbinder's apprentice
- Michael Faraday had little formal schooling and read the science books he was binding.
- He attended Humphry Davy's lectures, sent him his notes bound as a book, and got a job as his assistant.
- *He went on to discover electromagnetic induction.*
> Noether taught under a man's name

story 4 | Emmy Noether lectured under Hilbert's name
- Women couldn't hold university posts at Göttingen at first. Her courses were advertised as Hilbert's, with her "assisting".
- Hilbert protested: "We are a university, not a bathhouse."
- *She fled Nazi Germany in 1933 and taught at Bryn Mawr until her death in 1935.*
> Chandrasekhar worked it out on a ship

story 4 | Chandrasekhar's limit was calculated on a voyage
- In 1930, aged 19, sailing from India to England, Chandrasekhar combined relativity and quantum statistics for white dwarfs.
- He found a maximum mass: above it, stars must collapse further.
- *Eddington publicly mocked the idea. Chandrasekhar won the Nobel Prize in 1983.*
> Next: ideas that changed the world

## sc-ideas | Ideas that changed the world | philo | 0 | - | ph-aes
explain 4 | The scientific method wasn't invented in one go
- Aristotle's logic, Ibn al-Haytham's experiments, Bacon's induction, Galileo's maths, Newton's synthesis.
- Popper's falsifiability and modern statistics came much later.
- *It's a toolkit built over centuries, not a single recipe.*
> Germ theory

explain 4 | Germ theory replaced bad air
- For centuries, disease was blamed on miasma: bad air from rotting matter.
- Pasteur and Koch showed in the 1860s–1880s that specific microbes cause specific diseases.
- *Ironically, miasma theory still led to useful sewers, by getting rid of the smells and the germs.*
> Plate tectonics

explain 3 | The Earth's surface is a set of moving plates
- Accepted only in the 1960s, it explains earthquakes, volcanoes, mountain ranges and ocean trenches.
- Plates move about as fast as fingernails grow: a few centimetres a year.
- *India crashing into Asia is still raising the Himalayas.*
